now the latest on a deadly school  shooting in michigan a lawsuit accuses  officials of ignoring clear warning  signs fifteen-year-old ethan crumbly is  accused of killing four people in the  november 30th attack the lawsuit on  behalf of another student who was shot  at oxford high school includes  disturbing details of the suspect's  alleged behavior elise preston reports  on the lawsuit and crumley's first court  appearance in the case  ethan crumbly remained quiet as his  attorney entered a not guilty plea on 24  counts including murder and terrorism  charges  the michigan teen allegedly opened fire  in a hallway at oxford high school  killing four and wounding seven with a  gun that his parents bought him as an  early christmas present a civil lawsuit  against the oxford community school  district accuses administrators of  putting students in danger by allegedly  downplaying crumbly's actions before the  shooting when he posted countdowns and  threats of bodily harm at one point the  complaint claims that the boy left a  severed bird head in a mason jar  containing a yellow liquid in the boy's  bathroom  the school then emailed students and  parents that there has been no threat to  our building nor our students the  lawsuit also alleges that school  officials knew about these disturbing  drawings including a gun  the school knew that he made statements  such as he can't control his thoughts to  such a degree that the school was on  notice of a dangerous situation had a  duty of care to its other students to  make sure they were safe and simply  failed to act  along with school administrators  crumbly's parents james and jennifer are  also accused of ignoring warning signs  and allowing him access to the gun used  in the shooting they have been charged  with four counts of involuntary  manslaughter at a hearing on friday  prosecutors described texts sent by  ethan to his parents that they say  showed his mental state  ethan crumbly would text his mother  jennifer that he thought there was a  demon a ghost or someone else inside the  home  crumley's parents have both pleaded not  guilty their attorneys maintain that  they were receiving threats and left  their home due to safety concerns  meanwhile an attorney for the school  district tells cbs news some of the  claims in the lawsuit are false and  school officials are cooperating with  prosecutors tony  elise thank you very much
 

